Take the echo to be no louder than the microphone heard after the echo controller is aligned anew

After patch 0010's restart for recording, 0014's realign and 0016's restart for other
devices, AEC3 starts its linear filters and its delay over, and until the filter is
usable again (0.2 s after the delay is found, itself 0.4 to 0.6 s after the restart,
and a second start over when it is found) the residual echo is modelled from the render
alone with the echo path's default gain, 1: the echo taken to be as loud as the other
side's voice. With the other side talking that model sits 20 dB or more above the real
echo of a room, and the suppressor holds the person's own words down for the first
second after a change of output.

While the initial state after an alignment anew runs (AecState::AligningAnew, until 2.5
s of render active since the last delay found), the residual echo estimator now caps
the echo path gain of its non-linear model, and of the reverb it feeds, at four times
(6 dB over) the energy the microphone heard against the render's since the alignment,
once 0.4 s of render as loud as active render on average has been measured. What the
microphone heard holds the echo and whatever else the room and the person added, so the
cap is never under the echo heard so far; the person speaking only raises it. A cap
once set only falls until the initial state ends, and then goes. An echo controller made
anew, and every other change of the echo path, keep stock AEC3.

Fogata-Aec3EchoPathGainCapAfterAligningAnew/<x>/ sets the margin, from 0 (no cap, stock
AEC3) to 100; a value beyond that keeps the default, 4.

AEC3 alone at 16 kHz (the other side's speech-like sound, a room's echo 20 dB under it
at 60 ms, an output change every 8 s alternating with one at 180 ms, 300 ms or the same
delay, a word of the person's own 0.5 s and 3.5 s after each change, 8 runs): the word
at 0.5 s 3.9 to 4.0 dB down before, 3.2 after; with a second output 10 dB louder 5.9
before, 5.1 after; with the echo 10 dB under the other side 9.5 before, 9.1 after; with
the echo 30 dB under 3.7 before, 2.5 after. The word at 3.5 s and the echo let through
(the loudest 100 ms in the first 0.5 s, 0.4 to 1.3 s and 1.3 to 3.3 s after each change,
without the word) within 0.2 dB of before in every case. Starting the ERLE over sooner
after the alignment as well held the word at 3.5 s down by 0.8 dB more with the louder
echo, and is kept out.

